title: Testing the GUI end to end
tags: Development
kind: guide
audience: dev
aliases: gui-e2e
summary: scripts/dev/gui-e2e.sh runs the GUI's commands in a real window against a throwaway daemon, and checks what they said — never what they look like.

The Rust tests of the GUI drive its orchestration against a stub daemon, and the vitest
suites its JavaScript under jsdom ([[Testing conventions]]). Neither reaches the chain
between them: the shell's dispatcher, the Tauri `invoke`, the `#[tauri::command]`
wrapper. A command misnamed in the shell, or an argument the wrapper never receives,
passes both. `scripts/dev/gui-e2e.sh` runs that chain for real:

```
scripts/dev/gui-e2e.sh [--unsandboxed] [--keep-logs] [scenario...]
```

It boots an isolated daemon — the fixture the shipped scripts' integration tests use:
private token directory, empty configuration, ephemeral port — and a real
`metafolder-gui` on top of it, then gives each scenario a repository of its own, shown
in a fresh focused workspace. A scenario types commands with [[mf gui command]] and
reads back the outcome with [[mf gui status-bar]] (kind and text of the last status
message) and [[mf gui messages]] (the message log).

!! Its own display

The GUI runs on a private X server, `Xvfb`, when one is installed: its window never
shows on the user's desktop, and nothing the run does reaches the user's session. The
GUI is told to use X there (`GDK_BACKEND=x11`), Wayland session or not. Without
`Xvfb` it falls back on `$DISPLAY` or `$WAYLAND_DISPLAY`.

!! Keys pressed

Some of the GUI's wrappers are reached by no command, only by a key: the keybindings
panel's editor, the command input's history and its prompts. The keyboard
scenarios (`prompt`, `history`, `keybindings`, `open-with`) press those keys with
`xdotool`, in the GUI's window, and only on the private display. On a real one they
would type into whatever the user is doing. A desktop's own shortcuts can catch
keys too. On the first tries, on a real display under bépo, every `e` that `xdotool`
typed opened the file manager (Xfce binds Super+E to it) instead of reaching the GUI.
Later, with the same keymap, the same keys came through intact; the cause was not
found. On `Xvfb` nothing else listens to the keys, and its keymap is US. Without `Xvfb`
or `xdotool`, these scenarios are a loud `SKIP`.

A key changes nothing the scenario can read until the GUI has acted on it, and some of
those moments leave no trace: a command-input prompt opening, `ctrl+p` loading the
history. So a scenario arranges for a key pressed too early to do nothing:

* `Escape` with no prompt open runs nothing. The `open-with` scenario presses it until its command ends: once it ends, the prompt was there.
* A harmless command typed first: if the history's recall is late, `Return` runs `status:clear`. An empty line would run the first completion offered.

A letter pressed with no text field focused is a binding (`n` switches workspace, `t h`
opens the help). That is why a scenario never types text before a field is known to
hold the focus.

!! Clicks

What only a mouse reaches — a tab's close button, the value picker's button in a field
editor, the keybindings panel's ''Edit'' — is clicked through WebKit's remote inspector, never
at coordinates: a click aimed at a place moves with every change of style. The GUI runs with
`WEBKIT_INSPECTOR_HTTP_SERVER` on a loopback port of its own, and `scripts/dev/lib/webview.mjs`
(`node`) evaluates in the shell's page a search for the element, in the document and in
every panel's shadow root: `click <selector> <text> <inner selector> <inner text>` clicks
the one ''visible'' element matching the selector whose text contains the text (a hidden
workspace's panel is mounted too), or the one matching the inner selector inside it. A
scenario names a tab by its name and a keybinding's row by its key, not by their position.

Exactly one element must match, or nothing is clicked and the call fails: wrapped in
`until_ok`, it waits for the element to appear, and a selector gone stale fails, instead
of clicking something else.

The inspector answers any local process for the duration of the run, and the GUI it
reaches runs shell commands: a throwaway daemon, but a run belongs on a machine whose
other users are trusted. Without `node`, or when the inspector does not answer, the
clicking scenarios (`tab-close`, `pick-button`, `suggested-key`, `context-menu`) are a loud `SKIP`.

!! Its own configuration

The GUI reads a private copy of the installed configuration (`XDG_CONFIG_HOME` under
the work directory; the rendered docs are only linked), so a user's edits — a
keybinding, a user command — cannot change an outcome, and a scenario can install
what it needs: the `script` scenario's `e2e-ask.sh`, which asks one key with
[[mf gui input]]. What the GUI runs finds the run's own `mf` first on its `PATH`, and
this GUI through `METAFOLDER_GUI_URL`.

A command that runs a script (`script:run`) resolves when the script ends, so the
scenario starts it in the background and answers meanwhile; what the script says may
land just after the command resolved, and is waited for (`until_ok`), never assumed.

!! What is asserted

What a command ''said'', not how the window shows it: no pixel, no panel layout, and DOM
only where the GUI says something nowhere else — the keybindings panel shows its refusals in the
panel itself, not on the status bar. A change of style never breaks a scenario; a change of
wording or of behaviour does, which is the point. A click is named by selector, so renaming
a class it uses breaks one: the price of reaching what no command reaches.

Before each command the script posts a marker message of its own. A command that
reports nothing leaves the marker on the bar, so it fails instead of passing on the
message of the command before it.

!! Running it

It needs the binaries and the frontend built ([[Building]]), the configuration
installed (the GUI reads its panels from it — [[Updating the configuration]]), and
`Xvfb` or a display; the keyboard scenarios need `Xvfb` and `xdotool`. `--unsandboxed` passes `--allow-unsandboxed-webview`, for a container that
forbids WebKit's sandbox ([[Sandboxing the media decoders]]). A run takes about ten
seconds, most of it the GUI starting. It is not part of `scripts/dev/check.sh`, which runs
without a display. `METAFOLDER_BIN_DIR` (default `target/debug`) names the directory of
the binaries; [[Measuring coverage]] points it at instrumented ones, and the run ends
by quitting the GUI through its `quit` command — never a kill — so that their profiles
get written.

!! Confirmations

A confirmed command (`metarecord:remove`, `orphan:delete`, an `ignore:add` that would
shadow inherited patterns) is driven with `answering yes|no <command…>`, which passes
`--confirm` to [[mf gui command]] and keeps the questions asked in `LAST_QUESTIONS`. A
plain `run` answers `no` as well, and fails if anything was asked: a dialog nobody
answers blocks the shell's dispatcher, and every command after it would time out.

!! Adding a scenario

A `scenario_<name>` function: `new_repo <name>` for the repository and the workspace,
`df_mf` to prepare data through the daemon, `run` or `answering` to type a command,
then `status_is <name> <kind> <glob>`, `command_ok`, or the `assert_*` of
`scripts/dev/lib/assert.sh`. `gui_status <python expression>` evaluates one over [[mf gui status]] (`ws_count`, `slot_ws <slot>` are built on it). `select_path <abs path>` makes a path's metarecord the
selection (through the `viewer` panel); `show_panel <slot> <type> <command>` shows a
panel and waits until one of its commands answers. A keyboard scenario starts with
`needs_keyboard <name> || return 0`, then presses keys with `keys` (`xdotool` names:
`colon`, `Return`, `ctrl+p`) and types text with `typed`. A clicking one starts with
`needs_clicks <name> || return 0` and clicks with `until_ok 10 click …`;
`shows <selector> <text>` is true when exactly one such element is visible, `webview text …`
prints it. Add the name to the default
list at the top.

Name the target of a command that acts on the selection, even when the default would
do: the workspaces show the `message` panel because the metarecord list selects a
record of its own once loaded, at a moment no scenario controls. A value picker for a `ref` shows
one: the `pick-button` scenario waits for the list's cursor (`shows 'tr.row.cursor, …'`)
before it selects the record to pick, or the list's first row replaced it now and then.

!! Not covered

* The `sync` panel's conflict editor: a resolution is chosen in a row of the panel and saved with its button, which no command reaches. The `sync` scenario runs a plan without conflicts; a click could reach it now.
